{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310528,"nonce":"90StROA/B8gofugw2SMYNML1RDw=","title":"FIXED. `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.push(v)` silently lost f64 precision because the `array_push` MIR wrapper declared its value param as `IrType::Any` ...","message":"Two coordinated changes:\n\n1. **[stdlib/array.rs:362](compiler/src/stdlib/array.rs#L362)** — `build_array_push` MIR wrapper now declares value param as `IrType::I64` instead of `IrType::Any`. Body unchanged (still calls `haxe_array_push_i64`). Pointer-shaped values (Array\u003cString\u003e, etc.) still bitcast cleanly through the existing Ptr↔I64 coercion.\n\n2. **[stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs:1513](compiler/src/stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs#L1513)** — `Array.push` mapping uses the `mir_wrapper, types: \u0026[PtrVoid, I64]` form. This forces `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` to use the I64 signature instead of deriving it from the caller's arg types (which would give F64 for `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.push(v)`). The forward-ref's signature now matches the wrapper body's declared signature.\n\nThe existing F64↔I64 bitcast at [builder.rs:486-493](compiler/src/ir/builder.rs#L486-L493) (return side) and the F64↔I64 case at the call-site coercion (already in place) handle the actual register-level shift correctly. The bug was that the (F64, Any) pair never triggered any coercion because `Any` is opaque to the matcher.\n\n\n- The dispatch for `arr.push(v)` does NOT go through `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` (the forward-ref is created early in stdlib setup, not at the per-call site) NOR through `maybe_box_for_extern_call` (that's for a different dispatch arm).\n- The actual emission point is `add_instruction(IrInstruction::CallDirect)` reached via `build_call_direct`. Instrumenting `add_instruction` to log `CallDirect { func_id, args }` with each arg's IrType was the breakthrough — it showed `CallDirect array_push args=[Ptr(Void),F64]` against a function whose registered params were `[Ptr(U8), F64]`. The wrapper body had `[Any, Any]`. Three signatures, one function, deterministic precision loss.\n- The fix was the smallest possible coordinated change: align the three signatures via the I64 anchor in both build_array_push and the mapping.\n\n\n- `Array\u003cFloat\u003e` works correctly cross-context now. `floats.push(3.14)` round-trips through `floats[0]` exactly.\n- `Tensor.fromBytesF32` (added when this bug was a blocker) STAYS as the recommended path for bulk F32 loading — memcpy of N×4 bytes is faster than N method calls plus the coercion overhead, regardless of correctness. Just no longer required for correctness.\n- The companion `rayzor.Double` type proposed in conversation is no longer needed as a forcing function; `Array\u003cFloat\u003e` is the type-system-honest f64 storage now. Double could still be added as documentation/clarity but adds no runtime semantics.\n\n\nThe fix is targeted at `array_push`. Other Array operations that use the `Any` slot should be audited:\n\n- `array_set` (`arr[i] = v`): verified working pre-fix — but this is the indexer path which uses different runtime calls. Should re-verify with a dedicated test.\n- `array_unshift`, `array_insert`: same shape as push (write value into Any slot). Likely have the same bug — needs a regression test that round-trips f64 values through each.\n- `array_pop`, `array_shift`: read side. Returns I64 → caller expects F64. The return-side bitcast at builder.rs:486-493 should handle this, but worth confirming.\n- `Map\u003cK, V\u003e.set` where V is Float: same Any-typed value slot. Almost certainly broken; same fix shape applies.\n\n\n- [[bugs-gguf-q6k-and-f32-load]] — bug #3 in that note (\"Array\u003cFloat\u003e.push loses f64 precision\") is the one this memory closes. Update that note to mark this specific sub-bug as fixed.\n- [[bugs-null-arith-no-unbox]] — earlier fix for the binop arm of the same broader pattern (type-aware coercion at call boundaries).\n- [[bugs-null-int-cross-file-assign]] — Int variant of the same shape, still pending.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_array_float_push_any_param` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}